      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a12.8.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g12.7.6', 'g12.8.1'],
          lemma: "Ἔλεος θέλω … κύριος τοῦ σαββάτου",
          title: "Hungry men, holy bread, and the Sabbath's Lord",
          body: "The dispute is not whether Sabbath matters but what it is *for*, and Jesus argues it like a rabbi — from precedent. David and his men ate the twelve loaves of the Presence, bread reserved by law for priests alone (1 Samuel 21), and Scripture records no censure: human hunger outranked ritual restriction *inside the Bible itself*. The temple priests ”profane” every Sabbath by working it — slaughtering, baking, offering — and are guiltless, because temple service outranks Sabbath rest. Then the two escalations that turn a halakhic debate into a christological one: **something greater than the temple is here**, and **the Son of Man is lord of the Sabbath** — claims of a kind no rabbi made about himself, sitting calmly in an argument about snacks. Between them, Hosea 6:6 appears for the *second* time in this gospel (see 9:13), now sharpened from a reading assignment into a verdict: had they understood *mercy, not sacrifice*, ”you would not have condemned the guiltless.” Mark's parallel preserves the principle in its most portable form — *the Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath* — a one-line test that still works on any religious practice: does it serve the humans, or have the humans been conscripted to serve it?",
        },
      ],
