      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a12.20.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g12.20.2', 'g12.20.7'],
          lemma: "κάλαμον συντετριμμένον … λίνον τυφόμενον",
          title: "The bruised reed and the smouldering wick",
          body: "Matthew reads Jesus' habit of *withdrawing* — no shouting matches, healings hushed up — through the longest Old Testament quotation in his gospel: the first Servant Song (Isaiah 42:1-4). Two images from it have carried more pastoral weight than whole libraries. A **bruised reed** is a stalk creased but not severed — useless for anything, the natural move is to snap it off clean. A **smouldering wick** (λίνον τυφόμενον) is a lamp-wick burnt down to the smoking point, giving more smoke than light — the natural move is to pinch it out and trim a new one. The Servant's distinguishing mark is that he does neither. He does not finish off the damaged or extinguish the barely-burning; he handles them until the crease heals and the flame recovers, and the *until* clause matters — this gentleness is not going soft on justice but the chosen **method** of bringing justice through to victory. For a reader whose faith is precisely a smouldering wick — more smoke than fire, embarrassing next to the blazing confidence of others — this text is the specific promise that the one it describes does not put such things out. The whole of this project operates under that clause.",
        },
      ],
