          lemma: "Τίς ἐστιν ἡ μήτηρ μου; … τὸ θέλημα",
          title: "“Who is my mother?” — the door that swings both ways",
          body: "The chapter ends with its most uncomfortable scene, and the discomfort should not be sanded off. His mother and brothers stand outside — Mark's parallel says the family had come to take charge of him, convinced he was out of his mind — and Jesus, told they are asking for him, replies with a question that must have travelled back through the crowd like a slap: **who is my mother?** In a world where family was the deepest available loyalty (this gospel has already priced that: 10:37), he declines the claim of blood *as a trump card* — even his own. But watch the hand, not just the words: he stretches it out over the disciples — the fishermen, the tax man, the mixed bag of chapter 10 — and says *behold my mother and my brothers*. The sentence that wounds one family founds another, and its membership rule is startlingly open: **whoever does the will of my Father** — not whoever is born right, learned enough, or certified by the examiners who have stalked this whole chapter. The door swings both ways: blood does not keep you in, and nothing keeps you out. Worth adding: his actual mother made the second family too — she is there at the cross and at Pentecost (Acts 1:14), enrolled on the new terms like everyone else. The kingdom relativizes even its King's own household; what it asks of every comer is not pedigree but practice.",
